WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.180
Long distance sailors,
who brave months alone at sea,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.120
tend to report that
their greatest challenge

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.440
is the soul-destroying loneliness.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.540
We're prone to feeling lonely
when we're socially isolated,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.560
but we can also feel lonely
when we're not isolated,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.380
when we're in relationships
with people who care about us.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.180
So, what is that "terrible loneliness,"

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.320
as philosopher
Bertrand Russell puts it,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.660
where "one shivering consciousness
looks over the rim of the world

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.900
into the cold,
unfathomable, lifeless abyss."

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.200
Loneliness is the unwelcome feeling
that we lack companions,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.400
that we have fewer or poorer
relationships than we want.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.680
Feeling lonely is like feeling pain,
thirst and fear.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.440
It triggers our
"fight or flight" response.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.540
In small doses,
loneliness can help us.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.280
It can prod us to reach out
to other people,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.500
but when loneliness becomes
chronic and acute, it's corrosive.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.080
Some of us turn to drugs
to deal with loneliness.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.580
Some of us join abusive
relationships or gangs.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.540
It's correlated with health risks

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.560
such as depression, reduced immunity,
and even suicidal behaviour.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.180
Studies indicate that
young people feel lonely

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.900
as often as older people do.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.620
Even young children
can feel deep loneliness.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.220
If loneliness is a serious
social problem,

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.120
what do we do about it?

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.620
Can we have a right not to be lonely?

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.920
We cannot have a right
against feeling lonely,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.040
but we could have rights against

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.180
some of the underlying conditions
that tend to cause loneliness.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.360
Such as a right not to be
persistently socially isolated,

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.140
including a right not to be left
to fend for ourselves

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.940
}when we need help to stay social,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.880
like the physically impaired person

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.080
}who needs some help
to get out of the house.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.600
Although this isn't currently
an explicit right

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.140
in international agreements,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.700
arguably it should be,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.600
because human rights are about
the brute moral minimum

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.900
that we owe each other
as human beings.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.160
In addition to talking about rights,

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.580
we can work individually to alleviate
our own and each other's loneliness.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.140
There is value in small
social connections,

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.260
like the visit to the doctor,
the ride on the bus,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.180
and the trip to the grocery shop.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.220
We can make it a habit,
as writer George Monbiot suggests,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.660
to start conversations
with people we don't know.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.600
These micro-moments
of connection aren't just nice,

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.180
according to social psychologist
Barbara Fredrickson,

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.020
they change us for the better,
emotionally and physically.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.980
It's like getting exercise.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.860
And it goes both ways,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:55.500
our heart's capacity for love obeys
the biological law: Use it or lose it.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.920
As children's singer
Charlotte Diamond puts it:

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.600
Give four hugs a day -
that's the minimum, not the maximum.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.440
Thanks for watching! :)

